   "type": "section",
   "lesson": "01",
   "href": "lessons/01-design-is-a-loop.html#s01",
   "title": "The question is not the one it sounds like",
   "subtitle": "Lesson 01 · Ground floor",
   "text": "TAP Any word with a dotted underline opens a plain explanation in English and Vietnamese, plus where that thing shows up once a system is actually running. Nothing here assumes you have met it before. Here is what usually happens. The marker is barely in your hand and there is already a box labelled API , an arrow to a box labelled DB , and — because everyone has seen the diagram before — a third box labelled Cache and a fourth labelled Load balancer . Four boxes in forty seconds. It looks like progress. It is the opposite: you have drawn the answer to a question nobody asked yet. Because \"design a URL shortener\" is not one question. Shortening links for a team of twelve and shortening links for a social network are different products that happen to share a name. One of them is a weekend with a single database. The other has a redirect path that answers a hundred thousand times a second and a key generator that must never hand out the same key twice. You cannot know which one you are designing until you ask — and the asking is the first move, not a formality before the real work. So a design session is a loop . You ask what the system must do and how well; you turn the answers into a few numbers; the numbers tell you which boxes are earned and which are decoration; you go one level deeper on the single box that is genuinely hard; and you say out loud what you gave up. Then, very often, the deep dive changes a number, and you go round again. 1 · Requirements — what, and how well Shorten, redirect, list my links. Then the numbers: how many users, how fast a redirect, how long a link lives, how many nines. turn the answers into arithmetic 2 · Estimate — a minute of maths Users a day → requests a second → bytes a year. Good to a factor of three. Its only job is to point at the hard part. draw only what the numbers justify 3 · Sketch — five to eight boxes Every box can say which number put it there. A box that cannot is decoration, and decoration is where the bugs go to hide. pick the one box that is hard 4 · Deep dive — one level down, on one thing The redirect path, or the key generator, or the click counter. Data model, failure cases, ten times the load. Not all three; the one that matters. say what it cost 5 · Trade-offs — what you gave up \"The cache makes redirects fast and lets a deleted link keep working for a minute.\" No trade-off stated, nothing designed. and when the deep dive changes a number, back to move 2 The loop is the same in an interview, in a design document, and in the meeting where somebody asks whether the thing will hold up on launch day. The setting changes the amount of time you have. It never changes the moves. In production The loop leaves fingerprints. A design doc has a requirements section with numbers in it, a capacity paragraph, a diagram, one component with its own deep section, and a heading called \"Alternatives considered\". When one of those is missing, the system usually reveals which one during its first incident. Tiếng Việt \"Thiết kế một URL shortener\" không phải một câu hỏi mà là nhiều câu hỏi khác nhau, tuỳ quy mô. Vẽ ô ngay là trả lời một câu chưa ai hỏi. Thiết kế là một vòng lặp năm bước: hỏi yêu cầu, ước lượng, phác sơ đồ, đào sâu một chỗ khó, nêu đánh đổi — và bước sau có thể kéo bạn quay lại bước trước.",
