   "id": "01#s02",
   "type": "section",
   "lesson": "01",
   "href": "lessons/01-design-is-a-loop.html#s02",
   "title": "Five moves, run once on a URL shortener",
   "subtitle": "Lesson 01 · The idea",
   "text": "Move one — requirements. There are two kinds and they are worth keeping apart. A functional requirement is a verb the product does: take a long URL and return a short one; redirect anyone who opens the short one; let a user see the links they made. Three verbs. Everything else — custom aliases, expiry dates, click statistics — you ask about explicitly, because each one you accept adds a box later. A non-functional requirement is how well, and it has to carry a number: a redirect in under 100 milliseconds at the 99th percentile; available 99.9% of the time; links kept for five years. \"It should be fast\" is not a requirement. It is a hope. Move two — estimate. Say a million people use it daily and one in ten makes a link. That is 100,000 new links a day, which over 86,400 seconds is barely more than one write a second. If each link is opened a hundred times, that is about 116 reads a second on average — call it 350 at peak. Five years of links at 500 bytes each is under 100 gigabytes. The back-of-the-envelope estimate took a minute and it has already told you the most important thing about this system: at this size there is no hard part. One application server and one database will do it, and anybody who draws more is drawing for a different product. Now change one input. A hundred million daily users. Writes go to about 116 a second — still easy. Reads go to eleven thousand a second, thirty-five thousand at peak. That is a number one database should not be asked to answer on its own, and it is the number that earns the cache. Notice the order: the box appeared because the arithmetic said so, not because the diagram looked lonely without it. The read/write ratio did the deciding. Move three — sketch. Client, load balancer, a row of stateless application servers, a cache in front of a database. For the big version, a separate service that hands out unique keys, so that generating a key and checking it is unused never happens inside the write. Six boxes. Each one can name the number that put it there — which is exactly the test of a good high-level design . A box with no number behind it is not architecture. It is a guess wearing a rectangle. Move four — deep dive. Pick the one thing that is hard and go one level down. For the big shortener that is the redirect path: what exactly is in the cache, what happens on a miss, what the p99 latency looks like when the cache node restarts and every request falls through at once. Or it is the key generator: how does it never repeat, what happens when two servers ask for a key in the same microsecond, what if the service is down. You do not deep-dive everything. You deep-dive the bottleneck , because the bottleneck is the only place where getting it wrong changes what the user feels. Move five — trade-offs. This is where most sessions end early, and it is the move that separates a design from a drawing. The cache made redirects fast — and it means a link somebody just deleted keeps resolving until its entry expires. Pre-generating keys took the collision check off the write path — and it added a service that can itself run dry or fall over. Say each one in a sentence. A trade-off nobody said out loud is a surprise waiting for a production incident to introduce itself. In production The two kinds of requirement live in two different places once the system is real: the functional ones are the endpoints in the router, the non-functional ones are the SLO dashboard and the alert thresholds. If one of the numbers from move one is not on a dashboard, the system is not meeting it, whatever the doc says. Tiếng Việt Yêu cầu chức năng là động từ sản phẩm làm; yêu cầu phi chức năng là \"tốt đến mức nào\" và bắt buộc có con số. Ước lượng một phút cho biết phần nào khó — với một triệu người dùng thì không có phần khó, một server một database là đủ; với một trăm triệu thì đường redirect mới xứng đáng có cache. Ô nào trên sơ đồ cũng phải nói được con số nào đặt nó vào đó, và đánh đổi phải nói thành lời.",
