   "id": "01#s03",
   "type": "section",
   "lesson": "01",
   "href": "lessons/01-design-is-a-loop.html#s03",
   "title": "Three ways the loop gets skipped, and what each one costs",
   "subtitle": "Lesson 01 · The danger",
   "text": "Nobody skips the loop on purpose. It gets skipped because each move feels optional in the moment, and each skip has a specific bill that arrives later. Boxes before questions The forty-second diagram. Its cost is not that it is wrong — it may even be right — it is that you cannot tell. Without requirements, there is no test a diagram can fail, so the conversation drifts into taste: \"I'd put Redis there.\" \"I'd use Kafka.\" Nobody can win an argument about a system nobody has described. In an interview this reads as someone who has memorised diagrams; in a team it reads as someone who has already decided. An estimate you did not do Skip the arithmetic and every box becomes equally important, which means the deep dive lands on whichever part you happen to know best rather than the part that will break. The tell is a design that spends ten minutes on the database schema of a system whose database will be idle, and one sentence on the redirect path that carries 99% of the traffic. The estimate is what tells you where to spend your attention. Without it you spend it evenly, which is to say badly. A trade-off you never said Every choice in a design costs something, and the cost does not go away because it was not mentioned. A cache that nobody said could serve stale data is still serving stale data; the only difference is that the first person to find out is a user. The habit to build is mechanical: each time you add a box, finish the sentence \"…and the price of that is\". If you cannot finish it, you do not yet understand the box. The line you never cross Never draw a box you cannot attach a number to. A cache is earned by a read rate or a latency target. A queue is earned by work that must not block a user. A second database is earned by bytes or by writes per second. If the number that earns a box does not exist yet, leave the box off and write down the number at which you would add it. That sentence — \"at ten thousand reads a second we add a cache\" — is worth more than the box, because it is the box and the reason, and the reason is what the next engineer needs. Where the loop shows up Four rooms, one set of moves. The time available changes; the order does not. The interview — 45 minutes Five minutes of questions, three of arithmetic, ten on the sketch, fifteen on one deep dive, the rest on trade-offs and what you would do at ten times the load. Interviewers score the moves, not the picture. The design document — a week The same five headings. Requirements with numbers, capacity, diagram, one component in depth, alternatives considered. Reviewers who cannot find a heading assume the move was skipped, because it usually was. The estimate meeting — an hour \"Will this hold on launch day?\" is move two asked by somebody who does not call it that. Answer with the arithmetic, and with which box is closest to its limit. The incident review — the morning after Every postmortem is the loop run backwards: which number was wrong, which box was missing, which trade-off was never said. Running it forwards is cheaper. In production The unsaid trade-off is the most common root cause in postmortems that nobody writes down as a root cause. The report says \"cache served stale data\". The truth is \"we added a cache without finishing the sentence\". Tiếng Việt Ba cách bỏ qua vòng lặp: vẽ ô trước khi hỏi (không có gì để sơ đồ sai được, nên tranh luận thành chuyện khẩu vị); không ước lượng (mọi ô quan trọng như nhau, nên đào sâu nhầm chỗ); không nêu đánh đổi (cái giá vẫn có, chỉ là người dùng phát hiện ra trước bạn). Luật không được vượt: không vẽ ô nào mà không gắn được một con số vào nó.",
