   "subtitle": "Lesson 01 · See it",
   "text": "The knobs are move one — the questions you would ask. The numbers underneath are move two, done for you. The list of boxes is move three, and it is strict: a box only appears when a number earns it, and the row says which number. At the bottom, the one thing worth a deep dive, and the trade-off you would have to say out loud. Start with a million users and watch the honest answer be \"one server, one database\". Then push the users up by a hundred and see which box appears first and why. Turn on click analytics and notice that the hard part moves — a write has just landed on the hottest read path in the system. A team's internal tool A popular consumer app A social network's links Reset Daily active users how many people open a short link on a typical day — Links created per user per day most people only open links; a few make them — Reads per link the read/write ratio — how many times each link is opened — Years a link is kept storage is the only number that keeps growing after launch — Redirect latency target, p99 the one number users feel directly — Count every click for analytics New links per second — Redirects per second, at peak — Rows kept — Storage — Peak is taken as three times the daily average, a row as 500 bytes, and the thresholds are deliberately round — an estimate is good to a factor of two or three, so a threshold with more digits would be pretending. The maths lives in system-design/sim-01.js and has its own check: node tools/test-sd-01.mjs .",
