   "text": "\"Before I draw anything — how many users, and how fast does a redirect have to be?\" Say it in the first thirty seconds, every time. It signals that you know a shortener for twelve people and one for a hundred million are different products, and it buys you the numbers everything else depends on. \"Which number put this box here?\" Ask it of your own diagram before anyone else does. A box that can answer is architecture. A box that cannot is a guess, and the honest move is to erase it and write down the number at which it would come back. \"Reads or writes — which one is the system actually made of?\" The read/write ratio decides whether you optimise the path that stores or the path that serves. A shortener is a hundred to one for reads; a logging pipeline is the reverse. Get this wrong and every later decision optimises the quiet half. \"Which one part is hard? Let's spend the time there.\" The estimate points at it. Spending equal time on every box is the most common way to run out of time before reaching the part that will break, and reviewers notice the sentence about the redirect path far more than the paragraph about the schema. \"…and the price of that is?\" Finish the sentence for every box you add. If you cannot, you do not yet understand the box. If you can, you have just written the line the next engineer will need most. Tiếng Việt Bạn không cần thuộc sơ đồ nào cả. Chỉ cần đi đúng năm bước theo thứ tự, biết bước nào đang bị bỏ qua, và với mỗi ô trên bảng trả lời được hai câu: con số nào đặt nó vào đó, và cái giá của nó là gì.",
