   "id": "02#s01",
   "type": "section",
   "lesson": "02",
   "href": "lessons/02-where-200-milliseconds-go.html#s01",
   "title": "One tap, eight hops",
   "subtitle": "Lesson 02 · Ground floor",
   "text": "TAP Any word with a dotted underline opens a plain explanation in English and Vietnamese, plus where that thing shows up once a system is actually running. Nothing here assumes you have met it before. Follow one request from a phone in Hanoi to a server in Singapore and back. Before the server hears anything, the phone has to turn a name into an address — a DNS lookup — then open a connection with a TCP handshake, then agree on encryption keys with a TLS handshake . Each of those is a conversation, and a conversation with somebody 2,000 kilometres away takes at least one round trip : out and back, about 30 milliseconds each, because light in fibre is fast but not free. Only then does the actual request leave. It arrives at a load balancer , which spends half a millisecond choosing one of the application servers behind it. The server parses the request, checks who is asking, runs the business logic — a few milliseconds, on a normal day. It asks the database for a row: half a millisecond across the datacenter network, two milliseconds for an indexed lookup, half a millisecond back. The response is assembled and sent, and takes the same 30 milliseconds to reach the phone that the request took to leave it. Add it up and the shape is unmistakable. The trip across the wide-area network shows up once on a warm connection and four times on a cold one. The database shows up once per query. Everything else — the load balancer, the application code, the datacenter hops — is loose change. Two kinds of hop carry the time, and both of them are round trips. DNS · TCP · TLS — only on a cold connection Three conversations before the request can leave. About 20 ms, then one round trip, then one more. On the second request they are gone. the request finally leaves the phone Across the wide-area network — one round trip Hanoi to Singapore and back: ~30 ms. To Frankfurt and back: ~150. No code changes this number; only distance and the count of trips do. inside the datacenter now — sub-millisecond hops Load balancer · application server 0.5 ms to pick a server, ~3 ms to do the work. The part everyone wants to optimise, and the part that is already fast. once per query Database — a datacenter hop plus the query ~2.5 ms for an indexed read. Forty of them, one after another, is 100 ms — more than the ocean. the response takes the same road back In production The browser's network panel draws exactly this as a waterfall: DNS, connect, TLS, waiting, download. Server logs start at \"waiting\" and end before \"download\". The two never agree on how long a request took, and the browser is the one telling the truth, because it is measuring from where the user sits. Tiếng Việt Một request đi qua tám chặng: DNS, bắt tay TCP, bắt tay TLS (chỉ ở kết nối lạnh), băng qua mạng diện rộng, load balancer, mã ứng dụng, database, rồi quay về. Cộng lại thì hai loại chặng chiếm gần hết thời gian — chuyến đi-về qua mạng diện rộng và database — và cả hai đều là những vòng đi-về, không phải code của bạn.",
