   "id": "02#s02",
   "type": "section",
   "lesson": "02",
   "href": "lessons/02-where-200-milliseconds-go.html#s02",
   "title": "The unit of latency is the round trip, not the line of code",
   "subtitle": "Lesson 02 · The idea",
   "text": "Light travels about 200 kilometres per millisecond in fibre, and a packet rarely takes the straight line. Hanoi to Singapore is a round trip of roughly 30 ms; to Frankfurt, 150 or more. That number is a property of geography. You can shrink it in exactly two ways — put the server closer to the user, or cross the gap fewer times — and neither of those is a faster algorithm. That is why the count of round trips matters more than almost anything you do inside the server. A cold connection costs three extra conversations before the first byte of your request moves: DNS, TCP, TLS. On a connection kept open — keep-alive — the second request pays for none of them. For a user on the far side of an ocean, that single configuration flag is worth 300 milliseconds, more than every optimisation you could make to the code combined. The same arithmetic applies inside the datacenter, on a smaller scale. A round trip to the database is half a millisecond plus the query. That sounds like nothing, and it is nothing — once. A screen that lists forty orders and then fetches each order's customer with its own query makes forty-one trips, one after another. At 2.5 ms each, the database has just cost more than the ocean did. The database is not slow. It is being asked forty-one times when once would do. That pattern has a name, the N+1 query , and it is the most common performance bug in existence because it is invisible in code that looks perfectly reasonable. You cannot make light faster. You can only ask for fewer trips, or make them shorter. So the budget for a 200 ms request, written honestly, looks like this. One trip across the wide-area network: 30 to 150 ms, depending on where the user is. One trip to the database: 2 to 3 ms if the query is indexed, 40 or more if it is not. Load balancer, application code, serialisation: 3 or 4 ms in total. On a cold connection, add three more wide-area trips at the top. Everything you will ever be asked to \"make faster\" is one of those lines, and the first job is to find out which. There is one more number the average hides. Measure a thousand requests and most take 40 ms — but ten of them take 900. That is tail latency , and it comes from things that happen occasionally: a garbage-collection pause, a lock held a moment too long, a cache that had to be refilled, a retry. A user who loads a screen with ten requests on it meets the slow one about one time in ten. Design for the p99, not the mean, because the p99 is what a real person feels. In production Connection pools are keep-alive by another name: the HTTP client keeps a few sockets open to each host, the database driver keeps a few connections to the database, and the handshake happens once per pool rather than once per request. When the pool is missing or too small, the waterfall shows a handshake on every request and nobody can explain why the service is slow \"even though the code is fast\". Tiếng Việt Đơn vị của độ trễ là vòng đi-về, không phải dòng code. Khoảng cách địa lý không code nào rút ngắn được; chỉ có hai cách: đặt server gần hơn, hoặc đi qua khoảng cách đó ít lần hơn. Kết nối giữ mở (keep-alive) bỏ được ba vòng đi-về ở request thứ hai. Bên trong trung tâm dữ liệu cũng vậy: N+1 truy vấn biến database \"nhanh\" thành chặng chậm nhất. Và đo p99, không đo trung bình.",
