   "subtitle": "Lesson 02 · See it",
   "text": "Move the user further away, close the connection between requests, or make the screen fetch one row at a time, and watch which bar grows. The two biggest hops are named at the top; the note at the bottom says what you would actually do about them. Notice that the application code is 3 ms in every scenario. It is the one number here that never decides anything. A healthy request First visit from another continent A list screen with N+1 Reset Where the user is same city · same region · another continent — Queries this request makes one — or one per row on the screen — Connection already open (keep-alive) Queries use an index Fire the queries together instead of one after another The user waits — Biggest hop — Second biggest — Those two, share of the total — Round trips: 5 ms across a city, 30 across a region, 150 across an ocean; a cold DNS lookup 20 ms; TLS 1.3 at one round trip (1.2 would be two); 0.5 ms for a datacenter hop; 2 ms for an indexed query, 40 for a scan. Typical shapes, not measurements of any one system. The maths lives in system-design/sim-02.js and has its own check: node tools/test-sd-02.mjs .",
