   "href": "lessons/02-where-200-milliseconds-go.html#s07",
   "title": "Five questions worth asking",
   "subtitle": "Lesson 02 · Take away",
   "text": "\"Where does the time actually go? Show me the waterfall.\" The first sentence in any performance conversation. It moves the discussion from opinions about code to bars on a chart, and the two biggest bars are usually not where anyone was looking. \"How many round trips is that?\" Ask it of a cold connection, of a screen load, of a service that calls another service. Round trips are the unit; a design that makes fewer of them beats one that makes each one faster. \"Is that per request, or per row?\" The question that finds N+1 before it ships. A 2 ms query is nothing once and a hundred milliseconds forty times, and the code that does it looks perfectly clean. \"What's the p99, not the average?\" Averages are what components report; percentiles are what a person feels. A mean of 40 ms with a p99 of 900 is a slow system with a flattering dashboard. \"Which of these hops can we move closer, and which can we skip?\" The only two levers that work on the wide-area network. Everything else is 3 ms of application code that was never the problem. Tiếng Việt Bạn không cần biết hết về mạng. Chỉ cần nhìn được waterfall từ phía người dùng, đếm được số vòng đi-về, hỏi \"mỗi request hay mỗi dòng?\", và nhớ rằng hai chặng lớn nhất gần như không bao giờ là code của bạn.",
