   "id": "03#s01",
   "type": "section",
   "lesson": "03",
   "href": "lessons/03-numbers-you-must-be-able-to-estimate.html#s01",
   "title": "A guess and an estimate look the same and are not",
   "subtitle": "Lesson 03 · Ground floor",
   "text": "TAP Any word with a dotted underline opens a plain explanation in English and Vietnamese, plus where that thing shows up once a system is actually running. Nothing here assumes you have met it before. Somebody in a meeting says \"we'll need a big database\". Somebody else says \"we should be fine on one server\". Both sound like engineering. Neither is, yet — they are guesses, and the difference between a guess and an estimate is not confidence or experience. It is whether the inputs were written down and multiplied. An estimate starts from one number almost every product has: daily active users . It multiplies that by what a user does in a day — how many things they create, how many they look at — and by how big each of those things is. Four results fall out, and they are the same four for every system ever designed: writes a second, reads a second, bytes a year, and bits a second leaving the building. Then each of the four is held up against what one machine, or one network link, can do. The one that is furthest past its limit is the hard part. Sometimes none of them is, and that is the most useful answer of all. The whole thing is a minute of arithmetic, and it is worth more than an hour of architecture talk, because it turns \"we'll need a big database\" into \"we'll write two hundred rows a second and store forty terabytes a day — the database is fine and the disks are the problem\". Those are different projects. Only the arithmetic can tell you which one you are on. Start — users a day, and what each does 10 million people; each uploads 2 photos and looks at 50. Written down, with the assumptions labelled as assumptions. multiply, then divide by 86,400 — call it a hundred thousand Rates — writes and reads a second 20 million uploads a day is ~230 a second; 500 million views is ~5,800. Times three for peak. multiply by the size of one thing Bytes — storage a year, bits out of the door 20 million × 2 MB = 40 TB a day, 15 PB a year. 17,000 views a second × 200 KB thumbnails = 27 Gbps out. compare each to one machine, one link Verdict — the one that is furthest past its limit QPS is fine. Storage is fifteen hundred times what one machine holds in a year. That is the design. In production Every one of those four numbers has a graph once the system runs: requests a second on the load balancer, split by method; bytes on the storage dashboard; the egress line on the cloud bill. The estimate is a prediction of those four graphs. Comparing it to them a month after launch is how you find out whether you can estimate — and how your next estimate gets better. Tiếng Việt Đoán và ước lượng khác nhau ở một chỗ: đầu vào có được viết ra và nhân lên hay không. Mọi ước lượng đều bắt đầu từ số người dùng mỗi ngày, nhân với việc mỗi người làm, nhân với kích thước mỗi thứ — ra bốn con số: ghi/giây, đọc/giây, byte/năm, bit/giây đi ra. So từng con số với sức của một máy; con số vượt xa nhất là phần khó.",
