   "id": "03#s02",
   "type": "section",
   "lesson": "03",
   "href": "lessons/03-numbers-you-must-be-able-to-estimate.html#s02",
   "title": "A dozen constants, and the discipline of rounding",
   "subtitle": "Lesson 03 · The idea",
   "text": "There is a short list of numbers that make estimation possible, and it is short enough to know by heart. Not to three digits — to the power of ten. Every one of them is a ratio waiting to be used: how much slower a disk is than memory, how much slower a network is than a disk, how many seconds a day has. Main memory read — ~100 nanoseconds The baseline. Everything below is \"how many times slower than this\". SSD random read — ~100 microseconds A thousand times slower than memory. Tens of thousands of them a second — the IOPS figure. Round trip inside a datacenter — ~0.5 milliseconds Five thousand memory reads. The price of every call to another machine, before that machine does anything. Spinning disk seek — ~10 milliseconds A hundred times an SSD. A few hundred a second at most, which is why nobody puts a hot database on one any more. Round trip across a region — ~30 ms · across an ocean — ~150 ms Lesson 02. The only numbers here that no purchase order can change. Read 1 MB — memory ~0.1 ms · SSD ~1 ms · 1 Gbps network ~10 ms Size times medium. The reason a 2 MB photo costs more to serve than a 200-byte row costs to store. Seconds in a day — 86,400, call it 100,000 Per-day to per-second is \"divide by a hundred thousand\". 20 million a day is 200 a second. Close enough, every time. A thousand, a million, a billion — KB, MB, GB; a trillion — TB Powers of a thousand, and 2 10 , 2 20 , 2 30 are close enough to them that the difference never changes a design. One well-run machine — a few thousand simple requests a second · a few hundred writes · about 10 TB of disk Round on purpose. If your number is ten times one of these, you have found the hard part; if it is a tenth, you have not. With those in hand, the method is four steps and one habit. Write the inputs down — users, actions per user, size per action — and label each as measured or assumed. Multiply to get the four outputs. Multiply the rates by two or three for peak , because traffic is not spread evenly across a day and a launch is not a typical day. Compare each output to one machine. And the habit: round everything to one significant figure as you go. 20 million uploads over 86,400 seconds is 231.48 a second, and every digit after the 2 is a lie about how well you know the inputs. Say two hundred. The order of magnitude is the answer; the rest is noise. Two of the four outputs need a size to exist, and the size is where estimates most often go wrong by a factor of ten. A row with a few ids and timestamps is a couple of hundred bytes; a row with a text field is a kilobyte or more; a thumbnail is a couple of hundred kilobytes; a photo from a phone is two to five megabytes. The payload decides whether bytes are the hard part, and it is the input most worth checking against a real table before trusting. The last output is the one engineers forget and finance departments do not. Reads a second times bytes per read is bandwidth , and it is quoted in bits, so multiply by eight. Seventeen thousand thumbnail views a second at 200 KB each is 3.4 GB a second — 27 gigabits — out of the door, every second, at peak. No single link carries that, and even if one did, egress is the most expensive line on most cloud bills. The arithmetic that says \"the request rate is modest\" is the same arithmetic that says \"we need a CDN\", if you carry it one step further. An estimate without its inputs written down is a rumour with a number in it. One more multiplier belongs in the kit. Not every item is read equally: a fifth of them get four fifths of the traffic — the hot set . That is why a cache a tenth the size of the data can answer most requests, and it is how to size one: a day's worth of new items, times a fifth, times the size of each. And bytes a year is only a number on a disk once you know how many years — retention is a business decision, legal or product or cost, that the estimate has to ask about rather than assume. In production The one-machine limits above are the reason a good design doc's capacity section ends in zeros: \"~200 writes/s, ~6k reads/s, ~15 PB/yr\". A reviewer can check each of those in their head against the constants. A doc that says 231.48 writes a second is telling you its author did not understand that the inputs were guesses. Tiếng Việt Một tá hằng số nhớ theo bậc mười là đủ: bộ nhớ ~100 ns, SSD ~100 µs, một vòng trong datacenter ~0,5 ms, xuyên đại dương ~150 ms, một ngày ≈ 100.000 giây, một máy tốt chịu vài nghìn request/giây và ~10 TB. Phương pháp: viết đầu vào, nhân, nhân 2–3 cho đỉnh, so với một máy, và làm tròn về một chữ số có nghĩa. Đừng quên băng thông tính bằng bit — nhân tám.",
