   "id": "03#s03",
   "type": "section",
   "lesson": "03",
   "href": "lessons/03-numbers-you-must-be-able-to-estimate.html#s03",
   "title": "Four ways an estimate lies, and one sanity check",
   "subtitle": "Lesson 03 · The danger",
   "text": "Estimates go wrong in a small number of repeatable ways, and none of them is arithmetic. They are all about what was multiplied, what was forgotten, and how the answer was dressed. Precision theatre \"We will receive 231.48 uploads per second.\" The inputs were a guess at daily users and a guess at uploads per user, each probably wrong by half. Five significant figures on the product of two guesses is not rigour; it is a costume. Worse, it invites the reader to treat the number as measured, and to build to it. Round to one figure and say so — \"about two hundred, could be a hundred or four\" — and the estimate is honest about what it knows. The average day that never happens Dividing by 86,400 gives the rate on a perfectly flat day, and no day is flat. Evenings are busier than mornings; a sale, a launch or a news story can be ten times a normal hour. Design for two to three times the average as a matter of routine, and ask what the one-day-a-year peak looks like before deciding it is somebody else's problem. A system sized for the average is a system that fails on its most important day. The unit that quietly changed Per day where per second was meant. Megabytes where megabits were meant — a factor of eight, in the direction that makes the network look fine. Kilobytes where megabytes were meant, because \"the photo is about 2,000\" and nobody said of what. Every one of these is a factor of ten or more, and every one has shipped. Write the unit next to every number, including the intermediate ones, and the error has nowhere to hide. The size nobody checked \"A row is about 100 bytes\" — and then the row has a JSON column, and it is 4 KB, and the storage estimate was off by forty. The size of one thing is the input that is easiest to check and most often assumed. If a table exists, ask it for its average row size. If an object store exists, ask it for its average object. If neither exists yet, say \"assumed\" out loud and write down the number at which the assumption would change the design. The line you never cross Never present an estimate without a sanity check against something real. Every estimate should be held up against a system that already exists — one you run, or one whose scale is public knowledge. \"Six thousand reads a second\" means little on its own; \"about what our current API does at lunchtime\" or \"a hundredth of a large social network\" gives it a place in the world. If the estimate says a photo app for ten million people needs less storage than your laptop has, the estimate is wrong, and the check is the only thing that would have told you. Where the estimate shows up Four rooms where the same minute of arithmetic is the difference between an opinion and an answer. The interview — three minutes of the forty-five Say the inputs aloud, round hard, keep the units. Interviewers are not checking your multiplication; they are checking that you know which of the four outputs matters. The design review The capacity paragraph. A reviewer who cannot find it assumes the boxes on the diagram were not earned — because without the numbers, they were not. The launch-readiness meeting \"Will it hold?\" answered as: which of the four is closest to its limit, by how much, and what we do if the peak is three times what we assumed. The cloud bill Storage a month and egress a month are the estimate's last two lines with a currency attached. The arithmetic that finds the hard part also finds the expensive part, and they are usually the same line. In production A month after launch, put the estimate next to the graphs. Each of the four numbers will be off by some factor. The factor is the most valuable thing you learn that quarter, because it is the correction you apply to every estimate after it. Tiếng Việt Ước lượng sai ở bốn chỗ lặp đi lặp lại: giả vờ chính xác (năm chữ số trên tích của hai lần đoán), quên đỉnh (ngày nào cũng \"trung bình\"), lẫn đơn vị (ngày/giây, bit/byte, KB/MB), và kích thước không ai kiểm. Luật: không đưa ra ước lượng nào mà chưa đối chiếu với một hệ thống có thật.",
