   "lesson": "03",
   "href": "lessons/03-numbers-you-must-be-able-to-estimate.html#s04",
   "title": "Estimate any system in a minute",
   "subtitle": "Lesson 03 · See it",
   "text": "The knobs are the inputs — users, what each does, how big each thing is, how long it is kept. The four numbers underneath are the outputs, already multiplied by three for peak. The list is the working, one line per number, so that every result can be traced back to an input. At the bottom: which of the four is the hard part, and by how much. Try the four presets. The shortener fits on one machine on every axis. The photo app has an easy request rate and an impossible storage line. The chat app stores almost nothing and writes constantly. The sensor fleet is a system with no readers at all. URL shortener Photo sharing Chat app Sensor fleet Reset Daily active users or devices — anything that generates traffic — Writes per user per day things created: links, photos, messages, readings — Reads per user per day things looked at — Size of one write a row, a message, a photo — Size of one read what leaves the building per request — Years kept retention — ask, do not assume — Writes a second, at peak — Reads a second, at peak — Storage, a year — Out of the door, at peak — One machine is taken as 5,000 reads or 500 writes a second and 10 TB a year; one link as a gigabit. Peak is three times the average; the hot set is a fifth of a day's items. All round on purpose — the sim finds the order of magnitude, not the third digit. The maths lives in system-design/sim-03.js and has its own check: node tools/test-sd-03.mjs .",
