   "subtitle": "Lesson 03 · Take away",
   "text": "\"What are we assuming? Let's write the inputs down first.\" Turns a guess into an estimate in one move. Users, actions per user, size per action — three numbers, each labelled measured or assumed, and now everyone in the room is multiplying the same things. \"Per day, or per second?\" The unit question, asked every time a rate is mentioned. The factor between them is a hundred thousand, and it has been lost in more meetings than anyone will admit. \"And at peak — three times that?\" No day is flat. Sizing for the average is sizing for the day that never happens; the launch, the sale and the news story are the days the system is actually for. \"Which of the four is closest to its limit — reads, writes, bytes, or bandwidth?\" The question the whole exercise exists to answer. One of the four is the hard part; the other three are not worth a diagram box until the numbers say so. \"Does that match anything we already run?\" The sanity check. An estimate that cannot be placed next to a real system — yours or a public one — is a number with no way to be wrong, which is the same as no number. Tiếng Việt Bạn không cần nhớ chính xác hằng số nào. Chỉ cần nhớ chúng theo bậc mười, viết đầu vào ra trước khi nhân, giữ đơn vị bên cạnh mỗi con số, nhân ba cho đỉnh, và luôn hỏi: trong bốn con số, cái nào gần giới hạn nhất — và nó có khớp với thứ gì đang chạy thật không.",
