   "subtitle": "Lesson 04 · See it",
   "text": "Add servers, then choose where the session lives, then throw an event at it: a burst of ordinary traffic, one server dying, or a rolling deploy that restarts them all. Watch the login survive or not, and watch the load spread evenly or pile onto one box. Start where every system starts — one server, session in memory, nothing wrong. Add a second and the same choice that was invisible a moment ago decides whether your users stay logged in. Two servers, memory Sticky sessions Shared store Reset Servers behind the load balancer the number that was 1 yesterday — Where the session lives memory · sticky · shared store · signed token — What happens ordinary traffic · one server dies · a rolling deploy — Asked to log in again — Sessions lost for good — Load spread — Can any server answer? — \"Asked to log in again\" counts sessions lost outright plus users whose next request lands on a server that never had their note; with even balancing that is 1 − 1/servers. Sticky load uses a fixed skew so one server always runs hot. The maths lives in system-design/sim-04.js and has its own check: node tools/test-sd-04.mjs .",
