   "id": "05#s01",
   "type": "section",
   "lesson": "05",
   "href": "lessons/05-nines-and-consistency.html#s01",
   "title": "How much is a nine, and who is it a promise to",
   "subtitle": "Lesson 05 · Ground floor",
   "text": "TAP Any word with a dotted underline opens a plain explanation in English and Vietnamese, plus where that thing shows up once a system is actually running. Nothing here assumes you have met it before. \"The system should be highly available\" is not a requirement, for the reason lesson 01 gave: it carries no number. The number is a count of nines. 99% available sounds excellent and is a disaster — it allows three and a half days of downtime a year. 99.9% — three nines — is about nine hours a year, or 43 minutes a month. 99.99% is under an hour a year. 99.999% — five nines, the figure people reach for without thinking — is five minutes a year, and buying it is roughly ten times harder and more expensive than the nine below it. That measurement has three names, depending on who is being spoken to. The raw number — the fraction of requests that succeed — is an SLI , the indicator. The target you hold yourself to — \"99.9% over 30 days\" — is an SLO , the objective, an internal promise the team designs against. The same figure written into a customer contract with money attached, credits paid out if you miss it, is an SLA . The SLA is always set a little looser than the SLO, so that missing the target warns the team before it refunds the customer. And the nine has a mirror image that is more useful than the nine itself: the error budget . If the target is 99.9%, then 0.1% — about 43 minutes a month — is a budget of downtime you are allowed to spend, on risky deploys, on experiments, on the incident that was always going to happen. It reframes reliability from a wish into an amount. A team with budget left can move fast; a team that has spent it slows down and stops shipping until the month resets. The nine you promised and the risk you can take are the same number, read from two ends. 99% — two nines ~3.65 days of downtime a year. Fine for an internal tool; a catastrophe for anything a customer pays for. 99.9% — three nines ~8.8 hours a year, ~43 minutes a month. The common target for a serious product, and the budget most SLOs are written at. 99.99% — four nines ~53 minutes a year. Now every dependency and every deploy has to be careful; the cost step is real. 99.999% — five nines ~5 minutes a year. Rarely needed, often demanded, and each nine above costs about ten times the one below. In production The SLO is the number a launch-readiness meeting is really asking about, and the error budget is the number that should decide whether this week's risky change ships now or waits. A team that tracks neither is not being more relaxed about reliability; it is being reliable by luck, and finding out the target only after it has been missed. Tiếng Việt \"Sẵn sàng cao\" chưa phải yêu cầu — thiếu con số. Con số là số lượng số chín: 99% cho 3,65 ngày chết mỗi năm, 99,9% khoảng 43 phút mỗi tháng, 99,999% khoảng 5 phút mỗi năm và mỗi số chín thêm đắt gấp mười. Cùng con số đó có ba tên tuỳ người nghe: SLI (đo được), SLO (mục tiêu nội bộ), SLA (hợp đồng kèm tiền). Mặt kia của số chín là error budget — phần được phép chết, tiêu vào deploy mạo hiểm.",
