   "title": "The nine, the chain, and the partition",
   "subtitle": "Lesson 05 · See it",
   "text": "Two things to feel here. Move the target and watch downtime fall by tenfold per nine; add dependencies and watch a chain of good services add up to a worse one. Then split the network and choose which way to fall — refuse writes and stay correct, or accept them and reconcile later. There is no setting that gives you both during the split. That is not a limitation of the sim; it is the theorem. Money: refuse, stay correct Likes: accept, reconcile later Reset Availability target each nine is ten times less downtime and roughly ten times the cost — Services in the chain the request is only up if every one is — During a partition, this data chooses consistency (refuse) or availability (accept) — Downtime a year, one service — End to end, whole chain — Its downtime a year — Nines, end to end — Reachable side · writes attempted — — NETWORK SPLIT Far side · what readers see — — Downtime is (1 − target) × minutes in a year; the chain is the target raised to the number of services. The partition assumes 100 writes attempted on the reachable side during the split, with about 5% of AP writes genuinely colliding. The maths lives in system-design/sim-05.js and has its own check: node tools/test-sd-05.mjs .",
