   "text": "\"How many nines, and what does an hour of downtime actually cost us?\" Turns \"highly available\" into a number, and the number into a budget decision. The cost of downtime is what tells you whether three nines or five is the honest target — and five is almost never it. \"What's our error budget, and how much is left this month?\" The nine read from the other end. A budget with room means ship the risky change; a spent budget means slow down. It is the single number that should govern how fast a team moves. \"What's the end-to-end availability once you multiply the dependencies?\" Nines multiply downward. A chain of three-nines services is a two-nines system, and an SLO promised without doing this multiplication is already broken. You cannot be more available than your dependencies, combined. \"When you say consistent — do you mean strong, eventual, or read-your-own-writes?\" Three different promises, three different costs, one overloaded word. Ask it the first time and you save the argument where two people mean two things and both think they agree. \"During a partition, does this data refuse the write or accept it?\" The CAP choice, made on purpose. Money refuses and stays correct; a like button accepts and reconciles. The disaster is answering \"whatever the database does by default\" and finding out during the incident. Tiếng Việt Bạn không cần chứng minh định lý nào. Chỉ cần biến \"sẵn sàng\" thành số chín và số chín thành ngân sách, nhân chuỗi phụ thuộc trước khi hứa, hỏi \"nhất quán nghĩa nào\" mỗi lần nghe từ đó, và với mỗi loại dữ liệu chọn trước: lúc mạng đứt, từ chối write hay nhận write.",
